1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,540
After a plane crash, the wreckage can be
scattered across a field,

2
00:00:04,560 --> 00:00:07,270
a mountainside, or the
bottom of the ocean.

3
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:12,360
And investigators go looking for one
thing first: the black box.

4
00:00:12,420 --> 00:00:15,070
It's built to survive almost anything.

5
00:00:15,120 --> 00:00:17,670
Fire, impact, deep water.

6
00:00:17,980 --> 00:00:19,810
So here's the obvious fix.

7
00:00:19,900 --> 00:00:24,040
If the black box survives the crash, why
don't we just build the whole plane

8
00:00:24,060 --> 00:00:24,610
out of it?

9
00:00:24,780 --> 00:00:26,370
It sounds like common sense.

10
00:00:26,660 --> 00:00:29,920
But the answer says a lot about
how planes are built,

11
00:00:29,940 --> 00:00:34,330
what the black box really is, and what
actually keeps people alive.

12
00:00:34,460 --> 00:00:35,730
Let's start with the name.

13
00:00:35,820 --> 00:00:37,930
The black box isn't black.

14
00:00:38,020 --> 00:00:41,280
It's bright orange, often with
reflective stripes,

15
00:00:41,300 --> 00:00:43,370
so searchers can spot it in the wreckage.

16
00:00:43,640 --> 00:00:47,520
The idea came from an Australian
scientist named David Warren.

17
00:00:47,580 --> 00:00:51,560
In the 1950s he helped investigate
a crash of the Comet,

18
00:00:51,580 --> 00:00:53,570
the world's first jet airliner.

19
00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:56,060
Nobody knew what had happened
in the cockpit.

20
00:00:56,180 --> 00:01:00,900
So he imagined a recorder that would keep
the last moments of every flight.

21
00:01:00,920 --> 00:01:04,480
He built his first prototype in 1956.

22
00:01:04,500 --> 00:01:07,590
It took years before anyone
took it seriously.

23
00:01:07,640 --> 00:01:12,020
Today, flight recorders are required on
airliners all over the world.

24
00:01:12,040 --> 00:01:13,900
And there isn't just one box.

25
00:01:13,920 --> 00:01:15,000
There are two.

26
00:01:15,020 --> 00:01:18,610
The flight data recorder
stores numbers: speed,

27
00:01:18,820 --> 00:01:22,280
altitude, engine power, the
position of the controls,

28
00:01:22,300 --> 00:01:24,240
many times every second.

29
00:01:24,260 --> 00:01:28,510
The cockpit voice recorder stores
sound: the pilots' voices,

30
00:01:28,600 --> 00:01:32,080
alarms, switches, even the
noise of the engines.

31
00:01:32,100 --> 00:01:35,380
For years it only kept the last two hours.

32
00:01:35,400 --> 00:01:39,940
A new US rule requires twenty-five hours
on newly built airliners,

33
00:01:39,960 --> 00:01:43,780
and Europe already requires
twenty-five hours on large planes.

34
00:01:43,800 --> 00:01:46,820
Both sit near the back of the
plane, in the tail.

35
00:01:46,840 --> 00:01:51,190
In many crashes, the tail is the part
that takes the least damage.

36
00:01:51,280 --> 00:01:52,960
Now, what's inside?

37
00:01:52,980 --> 00:01:55,070
Not some magic material.

38
00:01:55,160 --> 00:01:57,900
Most of the box is ordinary electronics.

39
00:01:57,920 --> 00:02:02,210
The part that has to survive is
small: a few memory chips.

40
00:02:02,300 --> 00:02:05,080
They sit inside thick layers
of insulation,

41
00:02:05,100 --> 00:02:08,180
wrapped in a shell of titanium
or stainless steel.

42
00:02:08,200 --> 00:02:12,020
One maker's armored memory unit
weighs about two kilograms,

43
00:02:12,040 --> 00:02:14,350
just to protect a handful of chips.

44
00:02:14,380 --> 00:02:17,200
And that armor is tested brutally.

45
00:02:17,220 --> 00:02:21,320
It has to survive an impact of three
thousand four hundred g.

46
00:02:21,340 --> 00:02:26,080
A heavy weight with a steel spike is
dropped onto it from three meters.

47
00:02:26,100 --> 00:02:28,920
It's crushed under more than two tonnes.

48
00:02:28,940 --> 00:02:33,240
It sits in a fire at eleven hundred
degrees Celsius for an hour,

49
00:02:33,260 --> 00:02:37,480
then bakes at two hundred and sixty
degrees for ten more hours.

50
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:41,860
And it has to survive the water
pressure six kilometers down.

51
00:02:41,880 --> 00:02:45,540
If it ends up in the sea, a beacon on
the side starts pinging,

52
00:02:45,560 --> 00:02:47,940
for at least thirty days.

53
00:02:47,960 --> 00:02:50,080
That toughness has paid off.

54
00:02:50,100 --> 00:02:55,380
In 2009, Air France flight 447 crashed
into the Atlantic Ocean.

55
00:02:55,400 --> 00:02:59,100
The recorders lay on the sea floor
for almost two years,

56
00:02:59,120 --> 00:03:01,953
about three thousand nine
hundred meters down.

57
00:03:01,973 --> 00:03:06,220
When they were finally found in 2011,
investigators could still read the

58
00:03:06,240 --> 00:03:07,080
data.

59
00:03:07,100 --> 00:03:09,480
Reading a recorder is careful work.

60
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:12,200
In the lab, investigators open the armor,

61
00:03:12,220 --> 00:03:16,840
take out the memory, clean it, and copy
everything before anyone tries to

62
00:03:16,860 --> 00:03:17,860
analyze it.

63
00:03:17,880 --> 00:03:20,660
Then they rebuild the last
minutes of the flight,

64
00:03:20,680 --> 00:03:25,047
second by second, and match the numbers
to the voices in the cockpit.

65
00:03:25,067 --> 00:03:27,287
But black boxes aren't perfect.

66
00:03:27,307 --> 00:03:32,820
In 2014, Malaysia Airlines flight 370
disappeared over the Indian Ocean.

67
00:03:32,840 --> 00:03:35,240
Its recorders have never been found.

68
00:03:35,260 --> 00:03:39,080
That's one reason engineers are now working
on recorders that break away

69
00:03:39,100 --> 00:03:43,280
from the plane and float, and on sending
more flight data out while the

70
00:03:43,300 --> 00:03:45,080
plane is still in the air.

71
00:03:45,100 --> 00:03:48,133
So why don't we just build the
whole plane that way?

72
00:03:48,153 --> 00:03:52,427
Because the armor only works because
the thing inside is tiny.

73
00:03:52,447 --> 00:03:55,627
A few chips need a few
kilograms of protection.

74
00:03:55,647 --> 00:04:00,720
A plane is the opposite: a huge, mostly
empty tube full of people,

75
00:04:00,740 --> 00:04:02,200
bags and fuel.

76
00:04:02,220 --> 00:04:05,693
Every part of it is designed to be
as light as possible,

77
00:04:05,713 --> 00:04:09,573
because every extra kilogram has to
be lifted into the sky.

78
00:04:09,593 --> 00:04:13,840
Wrap all of it in armor that thick, and it
gets far too heavy to ever take

79
00:04:13,860 --> 00:04:14,440
off.

80
00:04:14,460 --> 00:04:18,400
And even if it could fly, it wouldn't
save the people inside.

81
00:04:18,420 --> 00:04:22,053
The black box survives three
thousand four hundred g.

82
00:04:22,073 --> 00:04:27,600
In 1954, an Air Force doctor named John Paul
Stapp rode a rocket sled that

83
00:04:27,620 --> 00:04:29,907
stopped in about a second and a half.

84
00:04:29,927 --> 00:04:34,660
He took about forty-six g, one of the
highest forces a person has survived

85
00:04:34,680 --> 00:04:36,140
on purpose.

86
00:04:36,160 --> 00:04:41,300
Three thousand four hundred g is far beyond
anything a human body can take.

87
00:04:41,320 --> 00:04:43,820
So what does keep people alive?

88
00:04:43,840 --> 00:04:45,020
Not armor.

89
00:04:45,040 --> 00:04:47,780
Planning for the crashes you can survive.

90
00:04:47,800 --> 00:04:52,260
Since the late 1980s, seats in newly
designed airliners have to pass crash

91
00:04:52,280 --> 00:04:54,100
tests at sixteen g.

92
00:04:54,120 --> 00:04:58,260
And before a new airliner is approved, the
maker has to show that a full

93
00:04:58,280 --> 00:05:01,170
plane can be emptied in ninety seconds.

94
00:05:01,300 --> 00:05:05,210
Rules like these are shaped by what
investigators learn from crashes.

95
00:05:05,480 --> 00:05:08,630
And the black box is one of
their best witnesses.

96
00:05:08,760 --> 00:05:12,270
So why don't we just build the whole
plane out of the black box?

97
00:05:12,360 --> 00:05:14,880
Because the black box isn't a material.

98
00:05:15,020 --> 00:05:17,800
It's a tiny, heavily armored memory.

99
00:05:17,920 --> 00:05:20,950
And its job isn't to survive
the crash for us.

100
00:05:21,180 --> 00:05:24,120
The black box doesn't make
crashes survivable.

101
00:05:24,280 --> 00:05:28,270
It makes them understandable, so the
next flight is safer.

